  "theorem_statement": "STRENGTHENED MASTER THEOREM (Parts CLXXX\u2013CLXXXVI):\n\nThe W(3,3) master ladder  \u03a6\u2086=7 \u2192 J\u207b\u00b9=8 \u2192 q\u00b3=27 \u2192 q\u2074=81 \u2192 dim(E\u2086)=78 \u2192 dim(E\u2088)=248\nis self-consistent across six independent geometric constructions:\n\n  CLXXX  (basis): N(q-1,1)=\u03a6\u2086=7; 1+\u03a6\u2086=J\u207b\u00b9=8; J\u2083(O)=27=q\u00b3; 3\u00d727=81=q\u2074;\n                   81=72+9=2kq+q\u00b2; E\u2086=72+2q=78; E\u2088=(78+8)+81+81=248.\n\n  CLXXXII (CCT/Hashimoto): the 480-arc directed shell of W(3,3) = V\u00d7K = 2q(q\u2074\u22121);\n           empire packet K\u2212\u03bc=8=J\u207b\u00b9; loop fraction numerator \u03bb=2 injects collinearity.\n\n  CLXXXIII (Jacobiator):   45 cubic triads = kq+q\u00b2=J\u00b7q\u00b2; q\u00b2=9 is the firewall sector;\n           oriented roots 2kq=72; E\u2086=72+2q; H\u2081=72+q\u00b2=81.\n\n  CLXXXIV (Heptad/Cayley): Fano points = \u03a6\u2086=7; +1 \u2192 Cayley carrier = J\u207b\u00b9=8;\n           J\u2083(O) = 3+3\u00d78 = 27 = q\u00b3; sign compatibility proved.\n\n  CLXXXV  (Quotient cubic): 45-point quotient geometry has 27=q\u00b3 lines;\n           135 incidences = 3\u00d745 cubic triads; three Albert copies \u2192 q\u2074=81.\n\n  CLXXXVI (Sporadic):      \u03c4=kq\u03a6\u2086=252; V_Suz=\u03a6\u2086\u03c4+\u03bbq\u00b2=1782;\n           196883=(v+\u03a6\u2086)(v+k+\u03a6\u2086)(\u03a6\u2081\u2082\u2212\u03bb); 196884=\u03c4C(40,2)+4q\u2074;\n           j=qE+f=744; G\u2080=E\u2086+A\u2082=86; Fi22\u219278; Th\u2192248.",
  "interpretive_note": "This synthesis does not replace the detailed proofs in CLXXX\u2013CLXXXVI.  It provides the shortest exact algebraic spine showing that each rung of the master ladder is supported by at least two independent constructions after welding.  The CLXXXIII/CLXXXV cross-bridge check (both yield the same 45 cubic-triad atom) is particularly significant: it confirms that the E\u2086 cubic representation and the quotient geometry are two faces of the same combinatorial object.  The highest-priority open task remains the Z[\u03b6\u2081\u2082] unified element proof (Langlands sprint).",
  "next_target": "The Z[\u03b6\u2081\u2082] Langlands claim (NOTES/LANGLANDS_SPRINT_MAY_2026.md): prove that a single element z \u2208 Z[\u03b6\u2081\u2082] has N_{Z[i]}(\u03c0_i(z))=137 and N_{Z[\u03c9]}(\u03c0_\u03c9(z)) \u2208 {7,13}, establishing \u03b1\u207b\u00b9, \u03b2\u2080, \u03b2\u2081/\u2082 as Frobenius eigenvalues of a single automorphic object."
